mising lines which I'm working with  for my research were derived from across  between zucchini cultivar Romulus and a  gray zucchini landrace from a national  plant germplasm system in the picture we  can see on the Left one of the advanced  lines compared to susceptible zucchini  fratty Dungey and these advanced lines  are partially resistant in that disease  program progresses more slowly and  symptoms are reduced but we still  observe plant mortality on many plants  of these genotypes  however they perform better than the  topic sessions from a previous study  that evaluated several hundred different  people germ plasm equations i've also  challenged these breeding lines with  seven diverse pathogen isolates and the  response to disease is consistent  indicating the absence of any isolate  specific interactions so one of the  goals of my research is to identify  low-side that control resistance to  photographer crown and regret and do  that I've developed an F to matthew  population we can see the parents and  the f1 on the left that's acceptable  parent is zucchini variety Dungey in the  middle we have the breeding line that I  previously identified as being most  resistant and on the right is the f1 the  figure on the right shows area under the  disease progress curve on the parents  and f1 area under the news progress  curve is a measurement of disease  progression over time in this case using  measurements of disease incidents every  other day for two three weeks following  inoculation we can see that f1 is not  intermediate between the parents but is  closer in phenotype to the sensible  parent and we can also see that the  differences between the parents are  robust to  differences in the mountain  concentration where even evacuating  partially resistant parent with  suspension of a hundred thousand zones  first four mill we observe less disease  than on the susceptible parent  inoculated with a suspension of 1000 per  mil so we expect the genetic  architecture of this trait to be complex  and with that in mind we are employing  Volks aggregate analysis with all  generally sequencing of the books to map  QTL the idea behind this is  straightforward the consists of  evaluating a large number of plants  identifying the phenotypic extremes in  this case the most susceptible and the  most resistant sequencing those DNA  pools from those bulks estimating allele  frequencies genome-wide and identifying  regions that if between the books so  there can be several advantages in some  cases over traditional QTL mapping where  all individuals are phenotyped and gene  typed separately that is because your  gene typing fear and vidual x' gene  typing costs are lesser phenotyping is  also simpler in the sense that instead  of assigning a precise if is explored  all individuals it just requires the  researcher to identify the fugitive  extremes there can be a statistical  challenge in separating signal in allele  frequency differences between bulks from  sampling noise from sequencing but  several methods have been developed that  increase the accuracy of allele  frequency estimates at snips by  borrowing information from neighboring  murders so this slide shows how I  implemented this approach in the f2  population I mentioned earlier I  evaluated over 13,000 f2 seedlings I  sprang them with a suspension of taffeta  cap to see those cores and identified  bulks not just as acceptable and  resistant but also a random bulk to  estimate baseline allele frequencies in  the population and attempted to have the  books  15% of the population selection was  applied in each 72 cell trade the plants  were so did so that corresponds to 11  plants in each tray being selected for  the books and then tissue from for the  plants was poked DNA was extracted and  I'm currently quantifying it and  standardizing across 96 trays to end up  with 6 DNA pools the 3 books from both  stages of phenotyping that will shortly  be submitted for sequencing I'm 