l method populations that I  will use to validate any QTL that I'm  able to detect in the books arrogant  experiment and to estimate their effects  and I also hope to continue to do some  additional microscopy experiments and  differential gene expression analysis to  further understand what makes a  resistant plant more resistant so with  that I'd like to thank the smart and  Core labs my other members of my  committee  Michael is Erick and Philip Messer Carla  plant in Missouri lab who I collaborate  with from these projects ignition and  smart lab Holly Lang Ella Reeves was the  undergraduate who worked on the  microscope experiments and funding from  a New York State Department of  Agriculture and markets before you know  it seems like you that board  diversification or I mean other issues  specific resistance like being issued to  there's there's evidence in pepper for  very specific resistance there's there's  not much evidence in tributes I  evaluated I looked at response these are  some distant Alliance mostly its  horizons from New York but also a couple  isil's from the west coast and the  response was the same in all cases  plants died do you have any idea what  the difference is in those plants was  there for IV or or just what the cause  was I I don't know in part I think  there's there's kind of maybe a  threshold in terms of infection and  there's variation in terms of how many  plants of the same gene type reach that  threshold and end up dying and we see  that in pepper as well but I'm  interested in maybe using quantitative  PCR to try to estimate adjoin abundance  and associate that with survival there's  no more questions  [Applause]  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu

